Ze-Reviewed-Against: github.com/vishvananda/netlink v1.3.1

XfrmState gains StateScope for Ze's private Linux 7.3 XFRMA_STATE_SCOPE
(42): a bounded ordered OR-set of 1..64 canonical xfrm_selector records,
preserving families, exact zero ports, masks, interfaces and NONINITIAL.
Both wire boundaries validate the complete payload, and readback owns its
records. RFC 4301 Section 5.2: "Then match the packet against the inbound
selectors identified by the SAD entry to verify that the received packet is
appropriate for the SA via which it was received."

GETDEFAULT reads XFRMA_ZE_CAPS (43), a read-only receipt with feature bits,
the selector limit, automatic guard-reference count and exact ABI 0x5a450001.
Absent, duplicate, foreign or malformed receipts establish no enforcement.
Individual feature predicates preserve staged K1 scope/quote receipts without
claiming fragment context or gathering support.

Its test, TestZeXfrmStateScopeWritesSelectorFamilies in
xfrm_state_scope_selectors_linux_test.go, checks that the scope writer
accepts the selector family cases of
netlink-xfrm-10-selector-prefix-family.patch and writes each byte for byte.
It reuses that patch's case table.

This patch is never proposed upstream. XFRMA_STATE_SCOPE, XFRMA_ZE_CAPS
and the capability receipt describe a private kernel ABI that only Ze's own
kernel implements, so no upstream release will contain them. It sorts last
so that every change upstream could take applies before it.

Needs netlink-xfrm-09-icmp-flag.patch and the patches it needs: the
StateScope field follows the ICMP field of XfrmState. Its test also needs
netlink-xfrm-10-selector-prefix-family.patch, whose case table it reads.

Not for upstream. It applies to upstream main after the series and does not
build there, where the package handle is a function call.

Delete this patch when Ze's private XFRM ABI moves out of the library into
Ze's own code.

